pentax-kernel is a modified Rust port of portions of KytyPS5-Performance.

The seven SystemService notice/identity/lifecycle roles additionally adapt
src/libs/libSystemService.cpp at revision
209dffe9032d72749257ce18680d506b891284de, independently verified Git blob
690b813be6ceac9c707e06c5fff6ec3c70d13acb, under the same GPLv2 terms.
PentaX modifications in 2026 preserve the one-byte configured notice flag,
source no-op setters, ignored opaque report input, exact string error ordering
and full-width capacity. The source's fixed system name "Kyty" becomes the
explicit PentaX profile "PentaX"; only its seven NUL-terminated bytes are
written. The additive skip_notice_screen setting defaults to false, following
emulatorConfig.h and Config::SkipNoticeScreen, and passes through the existing
owned configuration path. Checked atomic guest writes replace raw stores.
Only independently observed full-prefix notice-getter and splash aliases are
added alongside seven canonical registrations. No actual host splash/power/
termination operation or mutable source setter state is invented.

The seven thread-priority roles additionally adapt PthreadGetprio/Setprio,
the POSIX scheduling wrappers and GuestPriorityToHost/LowerCoreBoundThread
from src/kernel/pthread.cpp at revision
209dffe9032d72749257ce18680d506b891284de, Git blob
a7fa3003f534af01a119f117954ffe899c982351, under the same GPLv2 terms.
Their nine canonical registrations follow libKernel.cpp, Git blob
b02a4d77404f839371d96d1f549b53043e77d25e. PentaX modifications in 2026 retain
exact signed guest metadata, output ordering, source error domains and the
once-at-start single-core class demotion. Owned handles, bounded work and
atomic checked writes replace unchecked host thread pointers and stores.
Host priority calls are replaced with explicit fair cooperative guest turns:
4096/2048/1024 blocks for high/normal/low, preserving runnable round-robin
admission. These ratios are PentaX policy, not a claim of identical Windows
scheduling. POSIX setschedparam changes attributes only; running effective
priority changes through Setprio. No host scheduling policy is changed and
host-call failures are not fabricated. See upstream provenance.

