PentaX modifications, 2026-10-18:
  Added whole-span admission and bounded pre-effect metadata staging for
  subrange unmap/protect, retaining ProtectMemory's outward rounding and
  zero-length ordering. Split records preserve allocation identity, names
  and physical offsets; compatible pieces from the same allocation rejoin.
  Shared handles, work and scratch are admitted before backing mutation.
  The native adapter decommits anonymous pages while retaining reservation
  ownership; partial irreversible decommit is a fatal poisoned-owner stop.
  Added actual native physical sections and shared direct views at 16 KiB
  offsets. Placeholder ownership prevents address release during view changes;
  removing views preserves bytes until whole physical release. Private and
  section commitments share a bounded backing budget. Prospective output
  overlap checks include physical aliases. Added GetAllocatedSpan/FindMappings
  and ReleaseDirectMemoryInternal semantics for completely allocated physical
  spans, partial release and removal of all alias intersections. Fallible split
  metadata and shared resource totals precede the backing transaction. Native
  rollback retains section offsets, bytes and permissions until irreversible
  closure; failed restoration or partial closure poisons the owner. Retained
  partial section commitment stays charged until its last piece is freed.
  Pools, unowned unchecked-release no-op and GPU/cache modes remain unsupported.
  Added bounded owned directory snapshots, checked little-endian 512-byte
  block packing and exact source name hashes/record lengths/types. Logical
  dot entries require no host parent lookup; link classification uses each
  Windows entry's own attributes without following its target. Source int32
  getdirentries/getdents registrations retain exact scope and return width.
  Payload/base outputs preflight and commit together before the cursor;
  unsupported overlapping outputs and backing failures never become fake
  register results. EOF avoids unused buffer access. Independent directory
  descriptors retain their captured bytes for reads/seek/fstat, while stat
  enumerates a fresh bounded view and reports actual packed size. Aggregate
  retained directory storage, scratch, work and handles are bounded; allocation
  failure never publishes a partial descriptor. Directory pread remains an
  explicit unsupported source branch. No writable filesystem is introduced.
  Added explicit read-only directory capabilities alongside the finite file
  inventories. Caller-selected directory handles are reopened and requested
  files are resolved through pinned cap-std 4.0.3, without ambient guest-derived
  host paths or recursive admission discovery. Descriptor state, positional
  reads, actual metadata and transactional guest outputs share existing bounds.
  Path aliases/devices and ambiguous intake overlays are refused. Directory
  reachability is supported; directory enumeration/stat/descriptors remain
  unsupported rather than fabricating source packed-dirent sizes. The cap-std
  dependency's applicable full notices are included in binary distribution.
  Added invoke_library for the audited direct-library return signatures in
  fileSystem.h, memory.h, semaphore.h, eventFlag.h and pthread.h, with kernel
  errno values from errno.h. Supported int64 error returns sign-extend the
  source negative int; int32 results use PentaX's zero-extended RAX policy.
  Raw invoke keeps typed errno outcomes. Unsupported/unbound semantics,
  allocation, limits and checked backing failures do not become register
  results. Output-overlap admission remains an explicit Unsupported policy.
  Direct kernel calls do not write POSIX errno; raw-syscall flag conventions
  remain outside the library-call profile.
  Added the exact libkernel/Posix wrapper exports with source-specific -1
  widths, including the int64 fstat wrapper, and source stdio validation.
  Replaced host thread_local errno with a stable initially zero four-byte
  guest cell in a separately budgeted, protected internal RW/NX page. Only
  the bound initial guest thread is admitted; no host pointer or firmware
  TCB offset is exposed. Success preserves errno; non-errno delegate and
  backing failures remain typed stops. Two explicit library declarations
  preserve qualified scopes without NID-only or namespace fallback.
  Added checked POSIX once admission and four-byte state transitions. Callback
  data is not execution evidence: the prepared core/worker owner validates
  source, live permissions, actual nested-call identity and opaque return
  evidence before publishing DONE. Calls preserve the same stack/TLS/limits,
  and the callback's void result is ignored. Contended waits, signals and
  cancellation remain unsupported. No host function-pointer call is retained.
